### Plugin System

<hr style="width:100%;border-bottom:3px solid black;">

Since [forever-development is considered harmful]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/but-why#forever-development-considered-harmful), the plugin-system for `compose.mk` is based around a simple idea.

> A flexible "core" system + modules is the right design for most things.  

In fact, the plugin-system *is* a sophisticated approach to module-management.  

* *The simple case:* nothing is very different from vanilla Makefile `include`, and this is just a way to divide up behaviour.  

* *The more interesting case:* plugins / modules can use **just-in-time compilation**, so that components which are *written in [CMK-lang]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/compiler)* can be used (*=imported*) into classic Makefiles.


Especially thanks to JIT compilation, the difference between modules vs plugins or user-defined vs "official" plugins is just a matter of terminology.  A good rule of thumb is that:

**Plugins inject content into existing namespaces.**  See for example the [repl plugin]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/zero-config-tuis), which depends on other plugins, but ultimately extends the [TUI capabilities]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/embedded-tui) and the [`tux.*` namespace]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/api/#api-tux).

**Modules create new namespaces.** See for example the [CMK VM plugin]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/plugins/cmk-virtual-machine).

**Official Plugins** are tracked in an external repo[^1], but also exist as a git submodule in the [core repo]({{github.repo_url}}).  Important official plugins are also tested by the main test-suite.

**User-defined Plugins** are defined somewhere on the [plugins search path](#where-plugins-are-found).

### Plugin API & Usage

{{site.api_link('include.plugins', mkdocs)}} macro.  


```make
# in a Makefile or a .cmk program
$(call include.plugins, virtual-machine.cmk)
$(call include.plugins, polyglot.golang.cmk coroutines.cmk)
```

#### How plugins bind
<hr style="width:100%;border-bottom:3px solid black;">

The file **extension** decides how a plugin binds:

| Extension | Binds by | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `*.mk` | verbatim `include` (fast, copy-free) | pure-make plugins stay simple |
| `*.cmk` (and `.cmk.mk`/`.CMK`) | JIT-compiled (lowered) then included | a CMK-lang plugin, transpiled at include time |

`strict=1` (the default) errors on a missing plugin; `strict=0` logs and continues, which is handy
for conditional features:

```make
$(call include.plugins, optional.cmk strict=0)
```

#### Where plugins are found
<hr style="width:100%;border-bottom:3px solid black;">

Plugins resolve from [`CMK_PLUGINS_DIR`]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/config#general-environment-variables),
a **`:`-separated search path** (like `$PATH`) whose default
is the project-local `.cmk` directory.  Each element is tried in order and the first match wins, so a
project-local `.cmk` can override or extend a globally-installed set of plugins.  The **first** element
is also the single writable staging dir ([`CMK_MODULES_DIR`]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/config#general-environment-variables)) and stays working-directory-relative, so
plugins remain visible under [container dispatch]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/container-dispatch)
(which bind-mounts the working directory).  An absolute path may be passed explicitly:

```make
$(call include.plugins, prefix=path/to/plugins plain.mk hello.cmk)
```

The demo below loads a `.mk` and a `.cmk` plugin from one `prefix=` in a single call -- the `.mk` is
`include`d verbatim, the `.cmk` is lowered first.  It is shown as **twins**: the same program as a
CMK-lang driver and as a plain-`make` mirror (toggle the tabs).

{{site.embed_mirror('demos/cmk/plugin-system.cmk', 'demos/plugin-system.mk', github=github, default='left')}}

#### Idempotent loading
<hr style="width:100%;border-bottom:3px solid black;">

The import family keeps registries of what has loaded (`__plugins__` / `__modules__`), both **exported**
so a re-parsing child process inherits "what the parent already loaded".  Use `require` for an
idempotent, child-safe import (it skips a plugin already in the registry), and `assert` as a dependency
guard:

```make
$(call __plugins__.require, virtual-machine.cmk)   # import once; a no-op if already loaded
$(call __plugins__.assert, virtual-machine.cmk)    # error unless it is loaded
```

### Plugin Index
<hr style="width:100%;border-bottom:3px solid black;">

The plugins that ship under `.cmk/` are indexed below, generated directly from each plugin's
header.  Recall from [How plugins bind](#how-plugins-bind) that a `.cmk` plugin is JIT-compiled
at include-time while a `.mk` plugin is `include`d verbatim.  Each is also described in the
[`.cmk` repo's README](https://github.com/robot-wranglers/.cmk/blob/main/README.md).

